LRC as a cascade of conditional clearances; the cycle-exclusion crux (oracle-S545)

(1) CASCADE  |SAFE| = prod c_i  (conditional clearance of runner i | previous)
   generic n=5: c_i = ['3/5', '5/9', '13/25', '9/13']  -> prod = 3/25 (= |SAFE|)
   AP n=5 (tight): c_i = ['3/5', '2/3', '1/3', '0']  -> prod = 0 (= |SAFE|)
   (a zero factor = the runner whose clearance the previous ones made impossible.)

(2) CYCLE-EXCLUSION crux, n=4 (3 runners, one triple): 3-cycle at loneliest time
    <=> inside-debt (sum v_i EVEN, S531) <=> the hard even-sum class?
    triple       sum parity 3cyc@t*  #SCC  |SAFE| tight
    (1, 2, 3)      6   even    True     3       0  True
    (1, 2, 4)      7    odd   False     3     1/8 False
    (1, 3, 4)      8   even   False     3    1/24 False
    (1, 3, 5)      9    odd    True     3    1/10 False
    (2, 3, 5)     10   even   False     3    1/20 False
    ... CLAIM '3-cycle@t* <=> sum even (inside debt)' holds for 104/262 primitive triples (speeds<=13)

READING: the cascade telescopes |SAFE| = prod of conditional clearances; a tight
system has a ZERO factor (a runner the previous clearances trapped). The user's
hidden cycle-exclusion is the governor: a runner TRIPLE forms a 3-CYCLE exactly
when the inside-debt is active (S531) -- i.e. the cycle-exclusion is VIOLATED
precisely on the hard class. Transitive triples (cycle-exclusion holds) = clean
conditional clearance; 3-cycles = the trapped/inside-debt obstruction. So 'no
3-cycle propagates' = the cascade clears = loneliness; the cycles are the defect.
